$DIR/main.p:23:4: &node is not sendable: it is an interface that may hold a *Node (converted at main.p:23, package main), which contains shared[int], whose count is not atomic; use synced[T] (spec section 19.2)
    The value sent on c gives another task &node. The program converts a *Node
    to that interface type, and a *Node contains a shared[int], directly or
    through a field, element or pointer. Both tasks could then copy or drop that
    shared[int], changing its count without atomic operations.
    Go's garbage collector makes every pointer safe to share. Plutonium counts
    shared references without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one
    task.
    Use a synced[T] instead of the shared[T]: its count is atomic, so it may
    cross between tasks.
    (see "Sendable types" in the Plutonium spec, section 8.8)
    (run "pluto explain E2010" for a longer explanation)
